    "result_text": "The late percent is in, and all 4 Playwright tests in `bin/e2e` pass.\n\n**How it works**\n- **The setting:** the sheet stores a late percent, which starts at 0.00 (new migration `V21__sheet_late_pct.sql`). The Settings form has a \"Late percent\" field and a \"now\" display. Saving it writes `SETTING_CHANGED \u2026 key=late-pct value=<pct>`, then the ROLLUP/TOTALS lines for any dues that moved.\n- **The charge:** for an open claim past its due-by date, the charge is the late percent of its figure for every full 30 days overdue. The figure is the one a statement bound it to, or else the live figure raised to the floor. The charge is rounded half-to-even like due and added on top. It appears in the claim's due, the person's and sheet's due-totals, the overdue totals and the period totals.\n- **Statements** still bind only the figure under the charge, so the charge keeps moving with the sheet's today. The STATEMENT line's due-total includes whatever charge there is at that moment.\n- **Settling** freezes the charge into the claim's due, so `CLAIM_SETTLED \u2026 due=` includes it.\n\n**Two fixes the tests needed**\n- **Settling dropped the charge.** The server set the claim to SETTLED before working out its due, so the claim no longer counted as overdue and lost its charge. The due is now worked out first, in `ClaimService.changeStatus`.\n- **Saving an empty clerk limit wrote nothing.** Scenario 1 enters \"\" into an already-empty clerk-limit field and expects an audit line. That never changes the field's value, so the form treated it as untouched and didn't send it. The form now sends any field the user filled in or changed.\n\n**Behaviour changes you should know about**\n- Changing **terms** can now move dues (through the late charge), so it now records ROLLUP/TOTALS lines when they change. Before, it only wrote `SETTING_CHANGED`.\n- Re-entering a setting's current value now saves it again and writes another audit line. Before, a field you didn't edit was skipped.\n- The clerk-limit check on settling now compares the due including the late charge.\n\nThe front end (`core/claims.service.ts`) and the server (`RollupService`) each compute the charge separately, the same way the existing due logic is duplicated.",
